WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.560
Most people read books to get smarter.

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:06.920
They get more informed, but their life

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:10.200
stays exactly the same. But a few books

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:12.640
make you dangerously smart. They rewire

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:15.520
your brain and change how you think, how

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:17.800
you decide, and how you see what others

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:20.200
don't. I've been a CEO and board [music]

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:22.680
advisor to billion-dollar companies, and

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:25.360
I've distilled hundreds of titles down

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:28.480
to four that function as a toolkit for a

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:30.720
self-taught genius. But first, something

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:32.880
more valuable than the list itself. A

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:36.080
framework for finding the books that

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:38.840
actually rewire you. Every book is an

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:41.920
investment of time and attention. Most

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:44.120
of them entertain you, some inform you,

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:45.600
but a very few change [music] you.

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:47.840
Here's how to tell the difference.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:49.760
Before you commit to a book, ask [music]

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:52.640
three questions. I call them the three

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:55.240
gates. Gate number one, the operator.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:57.520
Will this book change how you think or

00:00:55.240 --> 00:01:01.200
just change what you think? If a book

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:04.519
can install new mental moves, new ways

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:06.600
to see, to reason, to decide, then it'll

00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:08.560
make you a better operator because it

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:10.280
will bring the kind of system upgrade

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:12.720
that we're talking about here. Gate

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:14.015
number two, the challenger. Does this

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:14.040
book make you

00:01:14.015 --> 00:01:15.960
>> [music]

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:18.280
>> uncomfortable? Does it challenge your

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:21.120
beliefs? You know, the best books don't

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:23.440
confirm what you know. They dismantle

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:26.520
everything you're certain about page by

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:28.440
page permanently. If you finish a book

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:30.520
and you feel validated, you've learned

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:33.200
nothing new. And gate number three, the

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:35.800
fire alarm. I always ask myself, will

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:37.960
this book stop me from doing dumb things

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:40.000
with confidence? Will it prevent an

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:41.960
expensive [music] mistake? Great books

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.920
hand you tools to stop you from

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:46.920
confusing confidence from competence. If

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:48.680
a book passes any of these three gates,

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:51.080
it will upgrade your inner operating

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:54.120
system. Now, of course, I love books

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:56.160
like Grit or Atomic [snorts] Habits or

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:58.040
So Good They Can't Ignore You. Great

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:59.560
books. They're brilliant for building

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:02.520
tactics and changing your daily

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:05.360
behavior. We want to go beyond just

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:08.440
behavioral shifts. We want to upgrade

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:11.680
and scale our entire way of thinking.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:13.920
Most books will change your speed. These

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:16.000
four will change your engine. Book

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:18.440
number one was a gut punch to me. It

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:21.520
forced me to ask a terrifying question.

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:24.240
Are my heroes visionaries or just

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:26.680
lottery winners? Nassim Taleb talks

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:29.200
about it in our first book, Fooled by

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:31.959
Randomness. He shows why randomness

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:33.880
manufactures geniuses and it changes the

00:02:31.959 --> 00:02:36.040
way we think about outcomes. [music]

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:38.800
Look at the math. Imagine you invite

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:41.680
10,000 traders from Wall Street to a

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:44.400
stadium. Once a year, they flip a coin.

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:46.440
Heads, they double their money. Tails,

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:48.959
they go bust, they go home. After 1

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:50.680
year, half of them will go home. 5,000

00:02:48.959 --> 00:02:53.959
will come back. If you keep doing this

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:55.400
for 5 years, then 5 years later, 312 of

00:02:53.959 --> 00:02:58.160
those traders will be left in the

00:02:55.400 --> 00:03:01.120
stadium. They won all five times. Now,

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:03.400
these 312 people will be on the cover of

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:05.320
Forbes. They'll write books. They'll

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:08.440
sell you systems. They'll start a

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:09.360
YouTube channel. But, are they masters?

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:12.120
No.

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:13.680
They are a statistical certainty. They

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:15.800
didn't beat the system. They were

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:18.280
produced by the system. That's why

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:21.120
Taleb's point is chilling. You cannot

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:23.440
distinguish a master from a lucky idiot

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:26.327
simply by looking at their track record.

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:28.560
At the AI company where I was CEO,

00:03:26.327 --> 00:03:30.680
[music] we were one of the fastest

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:33.040
growing firms in the US. I was

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:35.720
surrounded by some of the smartest

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:38.440
minds. Exceptional talent. But, we also

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:40.800
got a once-in-a-decade

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:43.560
tailwind we didn't control. The internet

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:46.360
was overwhelmed at that moment with the

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:48.800
cyber threat of fake transactions and

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:51.000
bots that acted like humans. It had

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:53.640
become an existential crisis for the

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:55.680
internet. So, we were the right company

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:58.160
at the right time. Was our team

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:00.200
exceptional? Sure. Did we work our butts

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:01.920
off? Of course. But, without that

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:05.080
existential threat, without that

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:06.720
tailwind, all of my visionary strategies

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:08.902
would have been a forgotten footnote

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:11.400
somewhere. Because we often confuse

00:04:08.902 --> 00:04:13.800
[music] a tailwind with our own talent.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:16.640
This is called the survivorship bias.

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:18.880
Stop trying to be a better forecaster of

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:21.920
your future. Start trying to be less

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:24.080
fragile, more resilient to randomness.

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:26.560
Now, what if you read this book already?

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:28.840
Then, the graduate-level upgrade would

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:30.919
be a book from a Nobel Prize winner,

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:33.520
Daniel Kahneman, Thinking, Fast and

00:04:30.919 --> 00:04:35.080
Slow. Kahneman shows you exactly what's

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:37.240
happening in your brain when you are

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:40.440
being fooled by randomness. His book

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:41.800
shows the exact wiring, system one, that

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:44.360
makes you jump to conclusions

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:46.680
immediately, and system two, which helps

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:48.720
us avoid those mistakes. How do you make

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:51.600
all of this actionable? Here are two

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:53.840
steps. First, the luck-labor audit.

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:56.560
Every Friday, audit your wins into two

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:59.280
columns. Column A, labor. What did I

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:02.240
control? And column B, luck. Timing,

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:04.400
tailwinds. If column B is empty, then

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:06.880
your ego is doing all the driving, and

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:09.160
you are headed for a crash. Second, the

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:11.480
wreckage tour. Now, this is my favorite.

00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:13.919
Stop studying just the survivors. Find

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:15.960
the people who made your exact bet, and

00:05:13.919 --> 00:05:18.240
then make it. Learn from them. Because

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:20.960
if your plan only works in the calm

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:22.880
seas, it's not a plan. It's a prayer.

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:25.160
Book number two is about the biggest

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:28.360
mistake successful people make. When you

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:30.520
win, you stop searching, and you somehow

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:32.760
start defending what you already know.

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:34.600
Adam Grant calls this out in his book,

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:37.480
Think Again. This is our second book.

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:39.480
The smarter you are, the harder it is to

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:42.240
change your mind. There was a study done

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:44.480
on tax professionals. Researchers gave

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:48.160
complex [music] tax code and problems to

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:50.920
both junior students and seasoned CPAs.

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:53.280
When the rules change just a little, the

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:55.520
experts failed three times more often

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:57.560
than the students because the experts

00:05:55.520 --> 00:06:00.080
were so certain. The more you know, the

00:05:57.560 --> 00:06:01.840
less you see. I call it the proficiency

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:03.920
prison. And the case studies are

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:05.640
everywhere. Kodak [music] invented

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:07.640
digital photography. They had the

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:09.840
technology, they had the talent, they

00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:12.000
had the resources, but they buried it.

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:13.880
Why? Because it threatened their film

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:15.880
business and Kodak's no more.

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:18.640
Blockbuster had a chance to buy Netflix

00:06:15.880 --> 00:06:20.680
for $50 million and they passed. Now,

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:22.520
none of these executives were stupid,

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:24.800
they were just trying to protect

00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:27.080
yesterday's wins. They were trapped in

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:29.120
their own proficiency prison. We wear

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:30.680
three masks when we don't want to change

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:32.720
our thinking. The preacher, the

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:34.960
prosecutor, and the politician. The

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:37.560
preacher defends the belief like it's a

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:39.760
scripture. Prosecutors will defend their

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:41.840
belief by spotting flaws in others. And

00:06:39.760 --> 00:06:43.840
politicians, well, they'll say whatever

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:46.440
the room wants to hear. Now, what do you

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:48.680
do to take these masks off? Be a

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:51.600
scientist. Treat your beliefs as

00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:54.240
hypotheses. Run experiments, update your

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:56.200
beliefs based on data. Now, if you read

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:58.320
this book, then the graduate level work

00:06:56.200 --> 00:07:01.440
is Innovator's Dilemma by Clayton

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:03.880
Christensen. That book shows you exactly

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:06.480
why companies love to stay in their

00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:07.760
proficiency prison. Intel dominated the

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:10.080
computer chip market for [music]

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:12.040
decades. They were so busy protecting

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:14.600
that business, they completely ignored

00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:18.320
the GPU revolution. Today, Intel is

00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:21.840
roughly a $220 billion giant, but Nvidia

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:23.320
is a $4 trillion ecosystem. And that is

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:25.560
what the Innovator's Dilemma is all

00:07:23.320 --> 00:07:27.600
about. The action plan here, there are

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:30.360
three mindsets that helped me a lot.

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:32.760
Scientist, stranger, startup. First, the

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:35.960
scientist mode. Take your strongest

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:38.720
current belief and rewrite it as I

00:07:35.960 --> 00:07:41.680
currently believe X, but it could be

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:44.280
wrong if [music] XYZ. It turns on the

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:46.280
scientist mode on demand. Second, the

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:48.840
stranger mode. Try to explain your

00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:50.360
strategy or plan to someone outside of

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:52.080
your industry, someone who doesn't know

00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:54.040
anything about it. Where they get

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:57.360
confused is exactly where you need to

00:07:54.040 --> 00:08:00.120
focus. And finally, the startup mode. I

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:02.160
never hire a C-level executive in any

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:04.160
company that I work with until I ask

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:06.400
them this very question. If you had a

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:08.520
million dollars and all the technology

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:11.320
in the world, what would you build to

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:13.880
take down your own company? If they can

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:15.440
map out their own destruction,

00:08:13.880 --> 00:08:18.360
they're scientists.

00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:21.280
If they defend the status quo,

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:22.800
they are just priests. The scientist is

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:24.240
never certain,

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:26.600
and the priest

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:28.560
is never curious. The third book

00:08:26.600 --> 00:08:32.000
completely changes how you judge your

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:34.159
own decisions. Annie Duke spent 20 years

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:36.520
being a world-class poker player. In her

00:08:34.159 --> 00:08:39.120
book, Thinking in Bets, she talks about

00:08:36.520 --> 00:08:41.385
this interesting way of thinking. Treat

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:41.560
every decision like a bet.

00:08:41.385 --> 00:08:45.160
>> [music]

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:48.440
>> When I was coming to Boston from Mumbai

00:08:45.160 --> 00:08:51.000
for my master study, the university had

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:52.680
only given me a conditional scholarship

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:55.160
because my scores were okay, they

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:57.600
weren't great. So, they said, you know,

00:08:55.160 --> 00:08:59.240
pay for the first semester yourself and

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:01.600
teach undergrad mathematics at the same

00:08:59.240 --> 00:09:03.560
time. And the department said, you know,

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:05.600
if you survive, we'll pay for the rest

00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:07.480
of the graduate program. And if not, you

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:09.920
have to pay out of pocket. And I said,

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:13.000
sure. What they didn't know was that if

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:15.320
I failed, I had [music] no way to pay

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:17.360
for the rest of the program. I thought

00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:19.480
in terms of bets. I outlined my

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:21.480
strengths, my weaknesses, I made a list

00:09:19.480 --> 00:09:23.560
of things that could go wrong, and I

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:26.800
calculated my odds. There was about a

00:09:23.560 --> 00:09:28.800
30% chance that I would fail. I was okay

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:31.200
taking that risk. I bought a one-way

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:32.480
ticket from Mumbai to Boston. It wasn't

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:34.920
bravery.

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:38.080
I really didn't have a plan B. The only

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:39.520
plan was to focus on increasing my odds

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:41.840
every day. [music] Now, of course, the

00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:43.960
bet could have gone the other way, but

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:46.040
the point is to know exactly what is at

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:47.720
risk and whether you have a plan to

00:09:46.040 --> 00:09:49.640
manage that risk. [music] And if you

00:09:47.720 --> 00:09:52.160
read this book already, the graduate

00:09:49.640 --> 00:09:55.480
version is Superforecasting. Philip

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:58.200
Tetlock studied 25,000 predictions over

00:09:55.480 --> 00:09:59.520
decades and found that the most accurate

00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:01.760
people on Earth, or the

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:04.560
superforecasters, don't think in terms

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:07.160
of yes or no. They think in terms of

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:09.640
bets, odds, possibilities, [music]

00:10:07.160 --> 00:10:11.560
probabilities, and they update that

00:10:09.640 --> 00:10:13.800
number every time new data hits the

00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:16.120
wire. They aren't trying to be right all

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:19.400
the time. All they're trying to do is to

00:10:16.120 --> 00:10:20.960
be less wrong after every iteration. So,

00:10:19.400 --> 00:10:23.320
how do you put this in action? The

00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:25.920
simplest one is to decision journal.

00:10:23.320 --> 00:10:27.440
Just do it for 30 days. I did it, super

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:30.320
helpful. For the next 30 days, for

00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:33.320
example, before any major decision or a

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:36.560
pivot, write down three things. One,

00:10:33.320 --> 00:10:38.880
your confidence level, 0 to 100%. Number

00:10:36.560 --> 00:10:41.160
two, what you know, what you don't, and

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:43.240
what evidence will change your odds. And

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:44.880
number three, write down three reasons

00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:47.440
why you could be wrong about this

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:50.160
decision. And track that for 30 days.

00:10:47.440 --> 00:10:52.480
Your emotions will get quieter, your

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:54.880
thinking will get [music] clearer. When

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:57.520
you start thinking in bets, you stop

00:10:54.880 --> 00:11:00.080
reacting and you start revising. Book

00:10:57.520 --> 00:11:02.160
number four is the most dangerous one on

00:11:00.080 --> 00:11:05.200
this list. Everything we have covered so

00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:08.080
far leads to this single question. What

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:10.839
if your intelligence itself is the trap?

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:13.080
David Robson spent years studying the

00:11:10.839 --> 00:11:15.320
relationship between intelligence and

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:17.680
cognitive failure. And the book he wrote

00:11:15.320 --> 00:11:20.720
was The Intelligence Trap. He shows that

00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:22.280
smart people have this special failure

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:23.960
mode because they can rationalize

00:11:22.280 --> 00:11:26.040
anything, and they become their own

00:11:23.960 --> 00:11:28.360
enemies because they become their own

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:30.600
defense attorneys. Most people hit a

00:11:28.360 --> 00:11:32.342
wall and question themselves. Smart

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:33.960
people hit a wall and question the wall.

00:11:32.342 --> 00:11:36.440
[music] And I've learned this the hard

00:11:33.960 --> 00:11:39.280
way. At my first job after coming out of

00:11:36.440 --> 00:11:42.040
MIT, I was the head of marketing for a

00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:45.040
global company. And in hindsight, I did

00:11:42.040 --> 00:11:47.560
not build an advisory board of mentors.

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:49.440
And I did not ask dumb questions early

00:11:47.560 --> 00:11:51.480
on. And when the company hired a new

00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:53.958
president, he could see that I wasn't

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:53.960
ready for a promotion. So, he brought in

00:11:53.958 --> 00:11:56.280
>> [music]

00:11:53.960 --> 00:11:58.560
>> an SVP of marketing instead of promoting

00:11:56.280 --> 00:12:01.520
me. And in hindsight, I think I had

00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:03.880
trapped myself in my own narrative about

00:12:01.520 --> 00:12:06.720
my capabilities, about my competence.

00:12:03.880 --> 00:12:09.640
And that's a very subtle trap. The book

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:11.680
helps you avoid such self-justification.

00:12:09.640 --> 00:12:14.440
The one drill that helps me the most is

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:17.160
what I call the anti-mentor. So, before

00:12:14.440 --> 00:12:19.880
any major decision, ask one person in

00:12:17.160 --> 00:12:22.680
your life whose judgment you trust to

00:12:19.880 --> 00:12:24.920
argue the opposite case as hard as they

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:27.160
can. Tell them, "Don't be polite. Try to

00:12:24.920 --> 00:12:30.000
kill the plan on paper." [music] And if

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:31.640
no one in your life can do that, that's

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:34.160
also a great problem to solve. The

00:12:31.640 --> 00:12:36.640
second practice is the empty [music]

00:12:34.160 --> 00:12:39.640
cup. Before your next high-stakes

00:12:36.640 --> 00:12:41.960
meeting, write down three naive

00:12:39.640 --> 00:12:44.200
questions you would be embarrassed to

00:12:41.960 --> 00:12:46.800
ask, and go ahead and ask anyway. There

00:12:44.200 --> 00:12:49.680
is a concept in the Zen Buddhism called

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:52.600
Shoshin, beginner's mind. The expert's

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:54.280
cup is always full, no room for anything

00:12:52.600 --> 00:12:56.440
new. The beginner's [music] cup is

00:12:54.280 --> 00:13:00.440
always empty, ready to receive

00:12:56.440 --> 00:13:02.920
everything. I saw this on a train ride

00:13:00.440 --> 00:13:05.280
many years ago. I remember we were going

00:13:02.920 --> 00:13:07.520
to New York City from New Jersey [music]

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:10.120
with our two kids, and they were little,

00:13:07.520 --> 00:13:11.520
seven and four. The train has to go

00:13:10.120 --> 00:13:13.680
through this tunnel because it goes

00:13:11.520 --> 00:13:16.320
under the river, and it was pitch dark

00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:18.880
outside, and [music] both kids were so

00:13:16.320 --> 00:13:23.360
incredibly excited to see it. They just

00:13:18.880 --> 00:13:24.360
started giggling with joy. Pure Shoshin.

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:27.280
Pure magic. [music]

00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:29.960
And that made me think. I took that

00:13:27.280 --> 00:13:30.899
train and went through the same tunnel

00:13:29.960 --> 00:13:31.000
twice a day

00:13:30.899 --> 00:13:34.520
>> [music]

00:13:31.000 --> 00:13:37.520
>> and never even once was I overwhelmed

00:13:34.520 --> 00:13:40.640
with this feeling of sheer joy. As we

00:13:37.520 --> 00:13:43.320
grow old, why do we treat our sense of

00:13:40.640 --> 00:13:46.480
wonder as [music] weakness and

00:13:43.320 --> 00:13:49.240
embarrassment? Why treat our curiosity

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:51.204
as if it's a curse? These books will

00:13:49.240 --> 00:13:53.800
help you reset your system so you

00:13:51.204 --> 00:13:57.040
[music] can answer those questions. But

00:13:53.800 --> 00:13:59.000
the ultimate system upgrade isn't adding

00:13:57.040 --> 00:14:00.920
more [music] to your cup.

00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:02.480
It's your ability to go through that

00:14:00.920 --> 00:14:05.520
dark tunnel

00:14:02.480 --> 00:14:07.400
and still find [music] magic in it.

00:14:05.520 --> 00:14:09.920
If you like this video, don't forget to

00:14:07.400 --> 00:14:11.640
subscribe and check out my most recent

00:14:09.920 --> 00:14:12.840
video here.

00:14:11.640 --> 00:14:15.520
Thank you

00:14:12.840 --> 00:14:15.520
and I love you.

